## New for this step: `RunDSL`'s `instance_eval` pattern has no Python equivalent

Ruby: `Boukensha.run(task: ...) do tool "read_file", ... end` works because
`RunDSL.new(registry).instance_eval(&block)` rebinds the block's `self` to
the `RunDSL` instance, so `tool` reads as a bare, receiver-less call.
Python has no `instance_eval`/blocks equivalent. **Decided with the user
(2026-07-23)**: the Python port uses an explicit callback function that
receives the `RunDSL` object as its argument —

```python
def setup(dsl):
    dsl.tool("read_file", description=..., parameters=..., block=read_file)

result = boukensha.run(task="...", setup=setup)
```

— rather than a context-manager-based deferred-execution design (the other
option considered). Slightly more verbose than Ruby's bare `tool(...)`
call, but explicit rather than magical, consistent with this project's
existing preference (e.g. `Registry#tool`'s `block=` keyword instead of an
implicit trailing block) for not manufacturing Ruby-specific implicitness
Python doesn't need.

## New for this step: `backend:` is a Ruby symbol with no Python equivalent

`Boukensha.run`'s `backend:` parameter and its internal `case backend
when :anthropic ... end` dispatch use Ruby symbols. Python has no symbol
type — `backend` is just a plain string (`"anthropic"`, `"openai"`, etc.)
throughout the Python port, matching every prior step's `provider` string
handling. `task_class.provider(task_settings).to_sym` becomes
`task_class.provider(task_settings)` with no conversion — `Player.provider`
already returns a plain string in Python, so there's nothing to convert
(same "not every Ruby line needs a Python counterpart" reasoning as
`02_the_registry`'s `transform_keys` gap).

## New for this step: several more load-bearing nil-vs-truthy instances

`Boukensha.run` has five separate `x ||= ...`/`x || ...` fallback patterns
(`system`, `model`, `backend`, `api_key`, `effective_max_output_tokens`).
All five need `is None` checks, not truthy ones, in Python — same
established reasoning as every prior step, listed here because this one
function has more of them in one place than any previous file. The
`ensure`/`logger&.close` cleanup is ported as a `finally` block starting
right before `Logger(...)` is constructed (not wrapping the entire
function) — behaviorally identical, since nothing before that point ever
has a logger open to clean up; not a narrower fidelity gap, just a cleaner
scope for the same guarantee.

## New for this step: `Logger` gains `turn`/`subscribe` — currently unused, ported for completeness

`Logger#turn(n:)` and `Logger#subscribe(&block)` (a pub/sub hook other
code can use to receive each log event as it's written) are both declared
in this step's Ruby source but **not called anywhere** in the actual
example or `Agent` — same "declared but currently unused" pattern as
`LoopError`/`quiet!`/`loud!` in earlier steps. Ported for completeness;
stay equally unused in Python.

One subtle detail worth preserving exactly, not "fixing": `write_log`
passes subscribers the **original** `event` dict, *without* the
`session_id`/`at` fields that get merged in only for the JSONL line
actually written to disk:

```ruby
def write_log(event)
  @log_io.puts JSON.generate(event.merge(session_id: @session_id, at: Time.now.iso8601))
  @log_io.flush
  @subscribers&.each { |s| s.call(event) }   # note: original `event`, not the merged record
end
```

Whether this is intentional or an oversight in the Ruby source isn't
something to unilaterally resolve — ported exactly as written.

Ruby's `@subscribers ||= []` lazy-inits on first `subscribe` call; Python
just initializes `self._subscribers = []` in `__init__` — a harmless
simplification (Python's `__init__` is already the natural single
init point Ruby's per-method `||=` pattern is working around), not a
behavior change (an empty list iterated in `write_log` is already a
no-op either way).

